## 2. Calibration — Owned by the Board Profile [CYD-TOUCH-CAL]

> **REWRITTEN 2026-08-14 — this section described a seam that has since moved.**
> It previously presented calibration as generic math to be applied wherever touch is
> read, and left axis inversion as a "test empirically and adjust the sign" instruction
> with no home. Calibration is now a **board property**, owned by the board profile behind
> the `[CXC-RM-M5]` seam. `touch_dispatch.cpp:76-78` states this at the point where the
> code used to be: *"Calibration moved to the board profile ([CXC-RM-M5] seam):
> `boards/classic_cyd.cpp` owns raw-ADC -> screen-px mapping; this dispatcher receives
> calibrated `TouchPoint`s."* The arithmetic below is unchanged from the original; where
> it LIVES is the correction. Drift baseline: `[DEC-SPEC-DRIFT-BASELINE-CYD-2026-08]`.

### 2.1 The Seam [CYD-TOUCH-CAL-SEAM]

`include/board_profile.h` defines the whole board-specific surface. Touch enters through
`TouchDriver` (`board_profile.h:107-112`), whose one output type is `TouchPoint`
(`:100-105`) carrying **screen pixels**, not ADC counts:

- `TouchDriver::read()` returns a calibrated `TouchPoint{float x, float y, uint16_t pressure, bool touched}`.
- `BoardProfile::touch()` (`board_profile.h:125`) returns `nullptr` on a board with no touch input — the absence of touch is a profile answer, not a `#ifdef`.
- The header states the rule directly (`board_profile.h:94-98`): the profile owns the raw-ADC-to-pixel mapping "because that mapping is a board property, not an engine property."

Everything above the seam — `main.cpp`, the band-rendering canvas, the config-driven scene
builder, and `touch_dispatch.cpp` — sees only calibrated `TouchPoint`s. Nothing above the
seam knows the panel has an ADC.

### 2.2 The Classic CYD Mapping [CYD-TOUCH-CAL-CLASSIC]

`boards/classic_cyd.cpp` is the reference profile. `ClassicCydTouch` (`:28-62`) wraps the
raw `XPT2046` driver and does the conversion in `read()` (`:32-58`), in this order:

1. **Early out** — an untouched reading returns `{0, 0, pressure, false}` without mapping (`:35`).
2. **Axis swap**, compile-time on `CYD_TOUCH_SWAP_XY` (`:38-44`): on this board raw X drives screen Y and raw Y drives screen X.
3. **Linear range map** to pixels (`:45-48`), the same arithmetic this section always carried.
4. **Axis inversion**, compile-time on `CYD_TOUCH_INVERT_X` / `CYD_TOUCH_INVERT_Y` (`:49-54`) — `s = SCREEN_DIM - 1 - s`. This is what the old "adjust the sign empirically" instruction became: a board constant, not a code edit.
5. **Clamp** to `[0, CYD_SCREEN_W-1]` / `[0, CYD_SCREEN_H-1]` via `std::max`/`std::min` (`:55-56`).

The constants remain in `cyd_pins.h` — the profile's data sheet — measured 2026-03-31
(`cyd_pins.h:88-94`):

| Constant | Value |
|----------|-------|
| `CYD_TOUCH_CAL_X_MIN` / `_X_MAX` | 353 / 3760 |
| `CYD_TOUCH_CAL_Y_MIN` / `_Y_MAX` | 300 / 3862 |
| `CYD_TOUCH_SWAP_XY` | 1 |
| `CYD_TOUCH_INVERT_X` / `_INVERT_Y` | 0 / 1 |

### 2.3 What This Buys [CYD-TOUCH-CAL-WHY]

Porting to a different panel is a new `boards/<name>.cpp` and nothing else — exactly one
profile translation unit is linked per firmware build (compile-time selection, the ESPHome
model) and it defines `board_profile()` (`board_profile.h:128-132`,
`classic_cyd.cpp:84-87`). The tree already carries three more profiles alongside the
classic one, and between them they exercise both ends of the seam:

| Profile | Touch | Evidence |
|---------|-------|----------|
| `boards/classic_cyd.cpp` | XPT2046 resistive, ADC + full calibration | `:28-62` |
| `boards/hosyond_s3.cpp` | FT6336G capacitive — **no calibration at all**, orientation only | `:284-287`, `:350` |
| `boards/qualia_rgb666.cpp` | none — `touch()` returns `nullptr` | `:372` |
| `boards/waveshare_c6_lcd147.cpp` | none — `touch()` returns `nullptr` | `:343` |

The Hosyond profile is the test the seam was built to pass: `HosyondTouch` is a
`TouchDriver` (`hosyond_s3.cpp:287`) wrapping a raw `FT6336` (`:331`, driver at
`include/touch_ft6336.h:62`) — a different wire protocol, no ADC, and therefore no
`CAL_MIN`/`CAL_MAX` concept — and nothing above the seam changed to accept it. Note the
layering: `XPT2046` and `FT6336` are both *raw* device drivers; neither derives from
`TouchDriver`. The profile supplies the adapter, which is where the board-specific mapping
belongs.

