## 3. Touch State Machine [CYD-TOUCH-STATE]

Debounce and tap detection:

```
         pressure > threshold
IDLE  ─────────────────────────►  DOWN
  ▲                                 │
  │          pressure < threshold   │  held < 500ms
  │          for 50ms               │  then released
  │◄────────────────────────────────┤
  │          (RELEASE → IDLE)       │
  │                                 ▼
  │◄──────────────────────────── TAP (fire on_tap)
```

| Parameter | Value | Rationale |
|-----------|-------|-----------|
| Pressure threshold | 200 | XPT2046 typical, tune empirically — `touch_xpt2046.h:18` (`kTouchPressureThreshold`) |
| Debounce time | 50ms | Reject noise, prevent double-tap — `touch_dispatch.cpp:100` (`kDebounceUs`) |
| Max tap duration | 500ms | Distinguish tap from long-press — `touch_dispatch.cpp:101` (`kMaxTapUs`) |
| Poll interval | **10 ms within a poll burst** — see correction below | `main.cpp:675-682` |

> **CORRECTED 2026-08-14 — the poll-rate row was stale.** It previously read
> *"Between frames (~3 Hz at current 340ms/frame render time)"*. That number was a
> consequence of a render-every-pass loop that no longer exists. Under the R3 policy
> (`main.cpp:647-654`) a static scene paints **nothing** when idle, so there is no
> per-frame cost to sample between: touch is sampled once per 10 ms `vTaskDelay` inside an
> explicit poll burst — 10 samples per pass on a static scene, 2 on an animated one — and
> the burst is cut short by a queued bus arrival (`main.cpp:680`). Frame time no longer
> gates the touch sample rate. Drift baseline: `[DEC-SPEC-DRIFT-BASELINE-CYD-2026-08]`.

The threshold comparison lives in the driver, not the state machine: `TouchReading.touched`
is set as `pressure > kTouchPressureThreshold` (`touch_xpt2046.cpp:229`), the board profile
carries it through onto `TouchPoint.touched` unchanged (`classic_cyd.cpp:34`), and
`process_touch()` branches only on that boolean (`touch_dispatch.cpp:120,134`). A profile
whose controller has no pressure reading (capacitive — §2.3) satisfies the same contract by
setting `touched` however its hardware reports contact.

State is tracked in the main loop, not in an ISR. The IRQ pin (GPIO 36, `cyd_pins.h:27`) is
available for interrupt-driven wakeup but is not used.

