## 5. Main Loop Integration [CYD-TOUCH-MAIN]

> **REWRITTEN 2026-08-14 — this section handed a builder code that no longer exists.**
> It previously showed `app_main` constructing a `static XPT2046 touch`, calling
> `register_widget(&gauge_temp, {5.0f, 30.0f, 115.0f, 115.0f})` and three siblings with
> hardcoded rects, and running a bare
> `while(true){ touch.read(); process_touch(); render_frame(); vTaskDelay(10); }`.
> **None of that is how the firmware works.** The touch driver comes from the board
> profile (§2), widget registration is derived from a YAML-configured scene tree, and the
> loop is event-driven with a render lock. Per the correction discipline this section now
> **describes the mechanism and cites source** rather than reproducing a snippet — a
> snippet is exactly what rotted here. Drift baseline:
> `[DEC-SPEC-DRIFT-BASELINE-CYD-2026-08]`. Line numbers are at commit `153b050`.

### 5.1 Touch Bring-Up [CYD-TOUCH-MAIN-INIT]

`app_main` does not construct a touch driver. It asks the board profile for one —
`board.touch()` — and initializes it only if the board has one (`main.cpp:641-645`); a
`nullptr` is a normal answer (§2.3) and every later use is guarded by the same pointer
(`main.cpp:677`).

**Ordering is load-bearing and is a board quirk, not a preference.** Touch init happens
*after* display init and after the first renders, because the ILI9341 init sequence
corrupts the shared SPI bus state for touch (`main.cpp:638-640`).

### 5.2 Registration Is Derived, Not Written [CYD-TOUCH-MAIN-REGISTER]

There are no hardcoded `register_widget` calls in `main.cpp`. The scene is built from
configuration and registration is derived from it in one call, `scene_register_touch(scene)`
(`main.cpp:609`, implementation `compose_scene.cpp:1649-1657`), which:

1. clears the registry, then
2. walks the scene's entries and registers **only those that are both `interactive` and `visible`**, with the rect the layout gave them.

`interactive` is a per-entry flag set by the scene builder when it instantiates a widget
type that responds to taps (`compose_scene.cpp:795,809,819,958,990`; field at
`compose_scene.h:64`). A builder adding an interactive widget therefore edits the scene
builder's widget table, never `main.cpp`.

**Registration is re-derived, not one-shot.** The old text's "call during scene setup,
before the render loop" is now only the first of three sites:

| When | Site |
|------|------|
| Initial scene build | `main.cpp:609` |
| A bus arrival flipped an interactive widget's visibility | `main.cpp:688-689`, gated on `scene_touch_dirty(scene)` (`compose_scene.cpp:1725`; the flag is set at `:324`) |
| A live config swap replaced the whole scene | `main.cpp:753`, inside the render lock |

`scene_free()` also clears the registry (`compose_scene.cpp:1668`), so a swapped-out scene
cannot leave dangling `Widget*` entries behind.

### 5.3 The Loop Is Event-Driven [CYD-TOUCH-MAIN-LOOP]

The loop lives at `main.cpp:659-759`. Its render policy is R3, stated in the code at
`main.cpp:647-654`: animated scenes keep a continuous full-frame cadence because sprites
need it; **static scenes are event-driven** — a bus arrival repaints only the dirty
band(s), a dispatched tap repaints the full frame (a tap may mutate state anywhere), and
**an idle loop paints nothing.**

Each pass, in order:

1. **Take the render lock** — `capture_http_lock()` (`main.cpp:662`). This serializes the whole loop body against the capture server's pull-render, which shares the band buffer and the SPI bus. This is the mutex §1.3 originally said was unnecessary; it became necessary when the HTTP capture server gained the ability to render on its own task.
2. **Touch poll burst** (`main.cpp:675-682`) — 2 iterations on an animated scene, 10 on a static one, each one `touch->read()` -> `process_touch()` then a 10 ms `vTaskDelay`. The burst exists because rendering blocks the loop and a short tap would otherwise fall between frames. **A queued bus arrival cuts the burst short** (`main.cpp:680`, `bus.pending()`); before that check existed, an arrival could wait 100 ms behind touch polling for a repaint that costs far less than that. One 10 ms tick always elapses regardless, so the loop can never spin.
3. **Drain the bus** — `drain_bus(scene, &arrivals)` (`main.cpp:687`, defined `:347`) returns a dirty-band mask; then re-register touch if visibility flipped (`:688-689`).
4. **Detect a tap** by comparing `tap_serial()` against the previous pass (`main.cpp:691-692`). `tap_serial()` is a monotonic counter bumped by the dispatcher on every dispatched tap (`touch_dispatch.cpp:155`, accessor `:112`) — the loop learns "a tap happened" without the dispatcher calling back into it.
5. **Render, or do not**: animated -> full frame every pass (`main.cpp:694-711`); else dirty -> `repaint_dirty_bands()` for the affected bands only (`:712-724`); else tapped -> full frame (`:725-727`); else **nothing is painted**.
6. **Release the lock** (`main.cpp:729`), then check for a config swap outside it, since a network fetch must not block the capture server (`:731-758`).

### 5.4 Consequence For Touch Latency [CYD-TOUCH-MAIN-LATENCY]

Touch responsiveness is now bounded by the poll burst (10 ms per sample) rather than by
frame time, which is what made [CXC-R-C9]'s "responds from frame 1" claim
(`main.cpp:644`) achievable. See the corrected poll-rate row in §3.

